use std
into soak.amni

Imports: math. Namespace soak. Wet fabric over time: rain reaches the surfaces it can see, soaks in, wicks along the cloth (more readily downhill) and dries. Module vars: `wet` (f32 per vertex, 0 dry to 1 soaked), `open` (f32 per vertex, how much rain reaches it, 0 to 1), `rep` (int per vertex: the welded vertex it belongs to), `nb_at` (int, per welded vertex plus one: start of its neighbors in `nb`), `nb` (int: neighbor welded vertices), `nb_w` (real per entry of `nb`: the downhill bias weight), `ry` (real per welded vertex: its y), `mw` (real per welded vertex: work wetness), `mo` (real per welded vertex: work openness), `grid` (real: the rain map's highest point per cell), and the rain frame as module vars `fw` (real, three: the direction toward the sky the rain comes from), `fa` and `fb` (real, three each: two directions perpendicular to `fw` and each other), `lo_a`, `lo_b` (real: the map's lowest a and b), `cell` (real: cell size), `gn` (int: cells per side).

`setup(P, I, fall real)` (P: read-only f32 slice, three per vertex; I: read-only u32 slice, three per triangle) prepares a mesh: resize `wet` and `open` to the vertex count, all 0. Weld vertices with exactly equal positions: a hash map from a key made of the three floats' bit patterns (each read as a u32, combined as x times 1000003 squared plus y times 1000003 plus z), whose value is a list of welded vertex numbers to compare positions against; `rep` gives each vertex its welded number, counting from 0 in order of first appearance, and `ry` the welded vertex's y. Neighbors: every triangle edge between two different welded vertices, each pair once (both directions), stored grouped by welded vertex in `nb_at` and `nb`. For each entry from welded vertex i to j, `nb_w` is 1 plus `fall` times the clamp to minus 1 to 1 of (y of i minus y of j) over the distance between them (0 when the distance is below 1e-12), then the larger of that and 0: water moves more readily to a lower neighbor. `mw` and `mo` are resized to the welded count, all 0.

`rain_map(dir_x real, dir_y real, dir_z real, lo_x real, lo_y real, lo_z real, hi_x real, hi_y real, hi_z real, size real)` starts a rain map for rain falling along the direction (dir_x, dir_y, dir_z) over the box from lo to hi with cells of `size`: `fw` is minus the direction scaled to length one; `fa` is the cross product of `fw` and (1, 0, 0) scaled to length one, or of `fw` and (0, 0, 1) when the absolute value of `fw`'s x is above 0.9; `fb` is the cross product of `fw` and `fa`. Project the box's 8 corners onto `fa` and `fb` (dot products) to get the lowest and highest a and b; `lo_a` and `lo_b` are the lowest minus 2 times `size`, `cell` is `size`, and `gn` is 1 plus the integer part of (the larger of the a span and the b span plus 4 times `size`) over `size`, at most 4096. `grid` is resized to `gn` times `gn`, all minus 1e30.

`cell_of(a real, b real) int` is the grid cell of a point: column the integer part of (a minus `lo_a`) over `cell`, row the same for b with `lo_b`, clamped to 0 to `gn` minus 1, as row times `gn` plus column.

`occlude(P, I)` (P: read-only f32 slice, three per vertex; I: read-only u32 slice) adds a mesh to the rain map. For each point, its a, b and h are the dot products of its position with `fa`, `fb` and `fw`. Every vertex raises its own cell to its h when higher. For every triangle with corners at (a, b, h): over the columns and rows from the cell of the triangle's lowest a and b to the cell of its highest a and b, take each cell's center (`lo_a` plus (column plus 0.5) times `cell`, and the same for b) and, when the center is inside the triangle in a and b (barycentric weights all at least minus 1e-9; skip triangles whose doubled area in a and b is below 1e-20), raise the cell to the h interpolated with those weights when higher.

`expose(P, N, tol real)` (P and N: read-only f32 slices, three per vertex) sets `open` for a mesh already added with `occlude`: a vertex is reached when its h is at least its cell's highest h minus `tol`; its `open` is then the larger of 0 and the dot product of its normal (scaled to length one) with `fw`, else 0. Then each welded vertex's `mo` is the largest `open` among its vertices, and every vertex's `open` becomes its welded vertex's `mo`, so seams agree.

`step(dt real, rate real, dry real, wick real)` advances wetness by `dt` seconds. Take substeps: n is 1 plus the integer part of `dt` times `wick` times 16, h is `dt` over n. Each welded vertex's `mw` is the largest `wet` among its vertices before the first substep. Each substep: soak, with each `mw` becoming `mw` plus h times `rate` times `mo` times (1 minus `mw`); wick, computing for every welded vertex i the change, the sum over its neighbor entries to j of h times `wick` times `nb_w` times (`mw` of i minus `mw` of j) when that is positive, which is taken from i and added to j (use a separate change array so every vertex reads the values from the start of the substep); then each `mw` is clamped to 0 to 1; dry, with each `mw` times the exponential of minus h times `dry`. Finally every vertex's `wet` is its welded vertex's `mw` as f32.

`mean() real` is the mean of `wet` (0 when empty).
